1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,888
Think of the heart as two pumps connected in series, not four
unrelated rooms. The venae cavae return systemic venous blood to the
right atrium. The right ventricle sends it through the pulmonary
artery to the lungs. Pulmonary veins return oxygenated blood to the
left atrium.

2
00:00:18,888 --> 00:00:35,976
The left ventricle then ejects through the aorta into the systemic
circulation. Its thicker muscular wall reflects the greater pressure
required for that task. Artery and vein describe direction of flow
relative to the heart, not whether the blood contains more oxygen.

3
00:00:35,976 --> 00:00:58,224
Follow a drop of blood through the right heart: right atrium,
tricuspid valve, right ventricle, pulmonic valve, pulmonary artery. On
its return: pulmonary veins, left atrium, mitral valve, left
ventricle, aortic valve, aorta. Say the sequence without swapping the
two sides.

4
00:00:58,224 --> 00:01:17,568
Valves move because the pressure gradient changes. During ventricular
contraction, AV valves close; papillary muscles and chordae restrain
their leaflets from prolapsing backwards. They do not actively open
the valves. Regurgitation means that the intended one-way barrier is
leaking.

5
00:01:17,568 --> 00:01:35,400
From inside outward, identify endocardium, myocardium and epicardium.
The epicardium is also the visceral layer of the pericardium. The
pericardial sac surrounds the heart, with a small lubricated space
that normally allows low-friction movement.

6
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:55,464
A problem outside the muscle can still compromise the pump.
Pericardial inflammation causes irritation, while pressure from a
significant effusion may impede filling. An ECG does not directly
measure the volume of that fluid or prove that filling is adequate;
bedside assessment remains essential.

7
00:01:55,464 --> 00:02:14,280
Ventricular diastole allows relaxation and filling. Ventricular
systole includes pressure generation and ejection. The shorthand that
semilunar valves are open during systole describes ejection; there are
also brief isovolumetric phases when all valves are closed.

8
00:02:14,280 --> 00:02:35,736
The first heart sound, S1, accompanies closure of the mitral and
tricuspid valves. S2 accompanies closure of the aortic and pulmonic
valves. Electrical depolarization triggers subsequent mechanical
activity, but a visible QRS does not guarantee an effective pulse or
adequate cardiac output.

9
00:02:35,736 --> 00:02:54,960
Coronary arteries arise near the aortic root and supply the working
heart muscle itself. The left main divides into the left anterior
descending and circumflex arteries. Use anterior and septal territory
for the LAD, and lateral territory for the circumflex, as an
introductory map.

10
00:02:54,960 --> 00:03:13,416
The right coronary artery often supplies the inferior left ventricular
wall and important nodal tissue. This helps explain why inferior
ischemia and slowed conduction may coexist. Coronary dominance varies,
so a single rhythm strip cannot establish which artery is obstructed.

11
00:03:13,416 --> 00:03:33,528
Gap junctions connect neighbouring cardiac cells so activation can
spread. Specialized conduction structures organize that spread into a
useful sequence. Without coordination, electrical activity may
continue while effective pumping fails. This is the bridge from
anatomy to rhythm interpretation.

12
00:03:33,528 --> 00:03:52,968
When you explain an ECG finding, complete the chain: where activation
begins, how it spreads, what contraction should follow, and what the
patient actually demonstrates. Pulse quality, mentation, skin and
pressure supply evidence that the electrical drawing alone cannot
provide.
